    "body": "<p>Humpback whales produce long, structured songs that can last tens of minutes and be repeated for hours, built from units grouped into phrases and themes in a hierarchy that researchers compare, cautiously, to the structure of human song. The songs were brought to public attention by Roger Payne&#x27;s 1970 album Songs of the Humpback Whale, and decades of recording show that the songs change gradually over a season and that populations share song versions that evolve over years. Sound travels far better in water than in air: low-frequency whale calls can cross whole ocean basins under the right conditions, which is why ocean bioacoustics studies both communication and the masking effect of shipping noise. Other whales contribute their own voices, from the patterned clicks of sperm whales to the varied whistles and calls of dolphins, making the ocean a deep, slow, long-distance biophony layer.</p><h4>Science proof</h4><p>Science proof: spectrogram analysis of humpback recordings shows repeatable phrase and theme structure that changes in a shared, directional way across a population over time, which is the evidence for cultural transmission of song. Hydrophone arrays have localised singing whales and tracked song exchange between populations. Playback and long-term monitoring studies link increased low-frequency shipping noise to changes in calling behaviour, including whales calling louder or shifting frequency, consistent with masking effects.</p><h4>Math proof</h4><p>Math proof: sound in seawater travels at roughly 1500 metres per second, so a call lasting 20 minutes covers a path length of 1500 x 1200 = 1,800,000 metres, or 1800 kilometres, if it travelled uninterrupted. The demo prints path length 1800.0 km for a 20-minute song, 900.0 km for 10 minutes, and the travel time for a 150-kilometre distance, 150000 / 1500 = 100.0 seconds.</p><h4>Soundscape layer(s)</h4><p>biophony</p><h4>Source</h4><p>Roger Payne, Songs of the Humpback Whale (1970); pu